  "_readme": "Surname notes. Two patterns dominate: office-names (a hereditary administrative or priestly post that became a family name) and village-names (the ancestral settlement, especially among Chitrapur Saraswats). Etymologies below are the explanations current in the community; several are folk derivations and should be read as tradition rather than settled philology. Corrections with a citation are very welcome.",
  "items": [
    { "name": "Kamath", "variants": ["Kamat", "Kamathe"], "communities": ["gsb"], "pattern": "office", "note": "Widely derived from a hereditary village revenue-accounting office." },
    { "name": "Pai", "variants": ["Pai Raiturkar", "Pey"], "communities": ["gsb"], "pattern": "title", "note": "An old title of standing; several derivations are proposed and none is settled." },
    { "name": "Prabhu", "variants": ["Prabhu Verlekar", "Pabhu"], "communities": ["gsb"], "pattern": "title", "note": "From Sanskrit prabhu, 'lord' — an honorific that hardened into a surname." },
    { "name": "Shenoy", "variants": ["Shenai", "Shanai", "Sinai", "Sinari"], "communities": ["gsb"], "pattern": "office", "note": "From an accountant's post; the Portuguese-era Goan spelling Sinai preserves the older sound." },
    { "name": "Nayak", "variants": ["Naik", "Naique"], "communities": ["gsb", "daivadnya"], "pattern": "office", "note": "A leader's or captain's title." },
    { "name": "Bhat", "variants": ["Bhatt", "Bhatta"], "communities": ["gsb", "chitrapur"], "pattern": "office", "note": "From Sanskrit bhaṭṭa, a learned brahmin; frequently a priestly family." },
